## Recipe 2 — Tangzhong Milk Bread (no egg, chewier and stringier crumb)

### Tangzhong (make first)

| Ingredient | Amount |
|---|---|
| Bread flour | 2 Tbsp |
| Milk | 1/3 cup |

Whisk them together in a small pan over medium-low heat until thickened to a pudding consistency with whisk lines visible, about 1–2 minutes. **Cool to room temperature** before using.

### Dough (in pan, in this order)

| Ingredient | Amount |
|---|---|
| Water | 1/4 cup |
| Milk | 1/3 cup |
| Tangzhong (cooled) | all of it |
| Bread flour | 2-1/4 cups |
| Sugar | 1-1/2 Tbsp |
| Salt | 1 tsp |
| Butter | 2 Tbsp, in pieces |
| Active dry yeast | 1 tsp |

**Dough check:** about 5 minutes into kneading, open the lid. The dough should be one smooth, slightly tacky ball. Add flour 1 tsp at a time if it's smearing on the sides of the pan, or milk 1 tsp at a time if it's dry or lumpy.

---

## Notes

- **Flour:** Spoon it into the cup and level it; don't scoop. For better pull-apart texture, use high-protein bread flour such as King Arthur (12.7%), or add 2 tsp vital wheat gluten to a generic bread flour.
- **Toast:** Slice about 1 inch thick. Freeze extra slices and toast them straight from frozen.
- **Wet (nama) panko:** Use day-old bread with the crusts removed. Tear it into chunks and pulse briefly in a food processor, or grate it on a coarse box grater (partly frozen bread grates more cleanly). Use it the same day or freeze it; it fries straight from frozen.